{"corpus_id":"paper-0012","review_status":"needs_repair","structured_code_summary_patch":{"mbse_objective_scope_artifacts.generality_reuse_mechanism":{"values":["template","reusable workflow","method guidance","high-level abstraction","low-dynamics / non-detailed-behavior model"],"status":"coded","rationale":"The reusable contribution is a baseline CubeSat mission-design architecture/workflow and guidance, not a fully mission-agnostic or interface-focused model. The paper explicitly says the architecture cannot be completely generalized but can be maintained as a baseline with modified inputs/requirements. Its generality also depends on high-level analytical abstractions and deliberate avoidance of detailed/numerical dynamic behavior. The current 'specific-mission model with reusable method only' is overstated because the paper demonstrates two generic use cases rather than one specific mission model.","evidence":[{"quote":"Since each space mission is unique, the model architecture introduced here cannot be completely generalized, however a baseline structure can be maintained with modifications of the inputs and requirements made for each use case.","location":"Section 1.1"},{"quote":"The general design structure matrix is shown in Figure 1... A set of inputs, which includes the mission requirements, feeds each of the mission disciplines, from which one or more performance metrics are produced.","location":"Section 1.1"},{"quote":"The models within the architecture exploit analytic methods where possible, in order avoid computationally expensive numerical propagation, and achieve rapid convergence.","location":"Abstract"},{"quote":"Since this work considers only long-term averages with respect to orbit trajectory, the position variables... remain undefined.","location":"Section 2.1"}],"confidence":"high"},"eps_verification_specific.ivv_integration_level":{"values":["executable/model-based checks"],"status":"coded","rationale":"The highest supported IVV integration level is executable/model-based feasibility or constraint checking inside the mission-design model. This should not be upgraded to trace-linked verification artifacts, simulation-in-the-loop, SIL/HIL, or formal EPS V&V. The evidence is model-executed parametric checks for feasibility, power sizing, and constraints before physical development.","evidence":[{"quote":"The process begins with the input of power requirements... Total power demand... Battery capacity required... Solar panel area required... Design is feasible... Select min. cost feasible design.","location":"Figure 2 description"},{"quote":"The battery capacity calculated in equation (5) is the minimum required to satisfy the demands from the platform, so the battery device would be a discrete size available that exceeds this.","location":"Section 3.1.3"},{"quote":"A penalty function is applied in this case to remove the solution from the Pareto set.","location":"Section 3.1.5"},{"quote":"These results were obtained in 10 minutes... 51,000 iterations of the model architecture.","location":"Section 4.2"}],"confidence":"medium"},"claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[].reliability_subtype":{"values":["mission assurance"],"status":"coded","rationale":"Only broad mission-assurance/robustness subtyping is supported. The paper does not directly claim EPS reliability, process reliability, or defect/error reduction. Non-reliability claims should remain 'not applicable' at claim level.","evidence":[{"quote":"A simple, dedicated method of directly relating cost and performance to key mission design parameters could significantly increase mission robustness...","location":"Introduction / claim C5"},{"quote":"The battery capacity calculated in equation (5) is the minimum required to satisfy the demands from the platform...","location":"Section 3.1.3 / claim C7"}],"confidence":"medium"}},"claim_level_reliability_subtype_patch":[{"claim_id":"C5","reliability_subtype":["mission assurance"],"rationale":"Mission robustness is a reliability-adjacent, mission-level assurance claim, but it is asserted without EPS reliability evidence."},{"claim_id":"C7","reliability_subtype":["mission assurance"],"rationale":"Power-sizing feasibility supports mission assurance, but the paper does not demonstrate EPS reliability, EPS fault tolerance, or EPS test evidence."}],"repair_notes":["Add 'low-dynamics / non-detailed-behavior model' to generality/reuse mechanism.","Remove 'specific-mission model with reusable method only' from generality/reuse mechanism.","Do not add 'mission-agnostic architecture' or 'interface-focused model'.","Keep IVV limited to 'executable/model-based checks'; no trace-linked V&V, SIL/HIL, or formal EPS verification procedure is shown.","Do not code EPS reliability; use only mission assurance for C5 and C7, with other claims not applicable."]}
